## a b s t r a c t
Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution from water splitting presents an attractive prospect in dealing with the energy crisis, but the low efficiency of charge separation and migration still seriously hinders its further practical application. Here, an acidified boron-doped $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ (HBCNN) and cobalt porphyrin metal organic frameworks (CoPMOF) self-assembled two-dimensional and two-dimensional (2D/2D) hybrid photocatalyst is fabricated successfully. The resultant HBCNN/CoPMOF with optimum ratio exhibits a superior $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of $33.17~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , which is 3.04 and 100.50 times higher than the single HBCNN and CoPMOF, respectively. It is found that a coordination connection has formed between CoPMOF and HBCNN through Co-N bond, and the interfacial Co-N bond then forms a pseudo-gap in the up-spin channel of electronic states, establishing an electron-hole separation mechanism. It is this electron-hole separation mechanism that contributes to a Z-scheme transport mode of photogenerated carriers, which greatly promotes the photocatalytic H2 production performance of HBCNN/CoPMOF heterostructure. This work may provide an idea for the design of heterojunction to improve the photocatalytic performance by constructing electron-hole separation through interfacial bond. $\circledcirc$ 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
Photocatalytic water splitting for hydrogen evolution presents a promising prospect to overcome energy crisis and benefits the sustainable development of civilization [1–4]. During the past few decades, immense amounts of photocatalysts have been developed, such as metal oxides $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ [5], metal sulfides (CdS) [6], metal oxyhalides (BiOX, ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}}={\boldsymbol{\mathrm{F}}},$ Cl, Br, I) [7], organic polymer (g$\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ [8], etc. Among them, $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ has been widely investigated due to the merits of low cost, convenient fabrication and tunable capacities. However, the photocatalytic performance of pristine $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ is still fairly limited by the serious recombination and slow mobility of photo-induced charge carriers.
For decades, two-dimensional and two-dimensional (2D/2D) layered structure has been widely considered as a valid approach to overcome the defects of charge separation and transfer process [9– 13]. Because the unique 2D/2D structure can cause more active sites and shorter transfer distance of charge carriers. Moreover, the close interfacial contact between two 2D components is in favor of the form of heterojunctions for more efficient separation of photoexcited charge carriers[14–18]. For example, Liu’s group has constructed a direct Z-scheme 2D/2D structure by loading $\mathrm{{MnIn}}_{2}S_{4}$ nanoflakes on $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanosheets, which leads to a tight interface contact, thus accelerating the migration and separation of carriers [19]. Bera’s team has fabricated a CdS nanosheets/rGO 2D/2D structure, which extends the optical harvesting edge of traditional broadband inorganic semi-conductor and simultaneously optimizes the transfer process of holes and electrons to active sites [20]. Chen et. al has designed a stable 2D/2D heterostructure of black phosphorus (BP) and oxygen-deficient bismuth tungstate $(\mathsf{B i}_{2}\mathsf{W O}_{6})$ , which solves the stability problems of BP and builds an S-scheme heterostructure to enhance charge transfer [21]. These successful reports demonstrate that constructing 2D/2D heterojunction is an effective method to inhibit the recombination of photogenerated carriers, and then improve photocatalytic performance.
In recent years, metal organic frameworks (MOFs), as a class of organic semiconductor materials, have aroused increasing research in various fields including photocatalysis due to their tunable porous structure, large surface area and high crystallinity [22–34]. Particularly, 2D MOFs have also attracted tremendous attention as a new member of 2D material family due to the merits of MOF. Compared with 3D MOFs, the reduced thickness of 2D MOFs can decrease the recombination of charges due to the directionless transfer in the rigid conjugation of metal nodes and organic ligands [35–36]. In addition, abundant number of coordinatively unsaturated sites are exposed on the surface of 2D MOFs like metal nodes with hanging bonds, which can contribute to improve the photocatalytic activity [37]. Recently, cobalt-based MOFs have become a research hotspot of photo/electrocatalytic reduction because of their strong reduction activity of Co sites [38]. For example, Yaghi et. al ever synthesized a cobalt porphyrin MOF $(A\mathsf{l}_{2}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}\mathsf{T C P P-}$ Co) for electrocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, and it was demonstrated that Co metal center is the major redox active site in the Co-MOF where ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{II}})$ is reduced to ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I}})$ causing enhanced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction performance [39]. Wang et. al predicted by DFT calculation that a Coporphyrin-based MOF linked to CoO-cluster can be used as an efficient photocatalyst due to the synergistic effect of CoO-cluster and Co-porphyrin [40]. Recently, carbon nitride $(\mathsf{g}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4})$ has been a famous semiconductor material with multiple merits, such as low cost, convenience of morphological and structural design, chemical and physical stability and eco-friendliness [41–42]. However, serious charge-carrier recombination hinders the industrial application of $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ , and the strategy of constructing hybrid structure with another proper semiconductor has been widely accepted to overcome this drawback. For example, Chen’s group loaded $\mathrm{MnIn}_{2}S_{4}$ nano-flakes on the surface of $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ to establish direct Z-scheme 2D/2D heterojunction resulting the accelerated transfer and separation of charge carriers [19]. Xu et al. synthesized $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 ( $\mathrm{Ti)}/\mathrm{g}{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{C}_{3}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}_{4}}/\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ni}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pd}}$ composite for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution and found the enhanced photocatalytic performance can be attributed to the intimate contact and accelerated charge transfer [43].
With these considerations, the construction of 2D Co-based MOF with 2D $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ may be a promising strategy to obtain photocatalyst with enhanced activity, and a few successful reports also demonstrate this point. For instance, Zhao et. al developed a 2D/2D heterojunction by combining protonated $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ with Smdoped sheet-like Co-MOF, which presented an enhanced photocatalytic activity for hydrogen evolution [44]. Lv et. al also successfully designed a $\mathrm{2D/2D~ZnCoMOF/g–C_{3}N_{4}}$ p-n heterojunction with enhanced performance for photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production [45]. However, due to the late attention, the 2D/2D heterojunctions composed of Co-based MOF and $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ are rarely reported. Furthermore, the deep mechanism of interfacial charge dynamics in those reported 2D Co-MOF and 2D $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ hybrids has never been revealed so far [46].
In current work, we successfully prepared a 2D/2D heterostructure consisted of HBCNN and CoPMOF for photocatalytic water splitting to produce hydrogen. The resultant HBCNN/CoPMOF 2D/2D heterostructure presented a significantly enhanced photocatalytic activity with a $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of 33.17 mmol $\bar{\mathbf{g}^{-1}}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ $\cdot5{\mathrm{~mg}}$ of catalyst), which is 3.04 and 100.50 times higher than the single HBCNN and CoPMOF, respectively. We found that interfacial Co-N bonds are formed between the N from edge sides of HBCNN and the coordinatively unsaturated Co from porphyrin rings of CoPMOF, which constructs a pseudo-gap in the up-spin channel of electronic states. The pseudo-gap confines the photogenerated electrons and holes in the CoPMOF and HBCNN, respectively. Thus, a new electron-hole separation mechanism is built, which contributes to a Z-scheme transport mode of photogenerated carriers, greatly improving the photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production performance of HBCNN/CoPMOF heterostructure.
## 2. Experimental section
Zirconium tetrachloride $\mathrm{(}\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\mathrm{)}$ , N, N-dimethyl formamide (DMF), benzoic acid (BA), urea, terephthalic acid (TA), sodium borohydride $(N a B H_{4})$ , ascorbic acid, hydrochloric acid (HCl) were obtained from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. China, and tetrakis (4-carboxylphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP) was obtained from Zhengzhou Ruke Chemical Products Co., Ltd. China. All reagents were analytical grade and used as received without further purification. Deionized water with resistivity of $18.2~\mathrm{M}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}$ was used throughout the experiments.
## 2.1. Preparation of photocatalysts
The preparation procedure of HBCNN/CoPMOF was illustrated in Scheme 1, including the preparation of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets, the boron doping of $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ nanosheets, the acid treatment of boron doped $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanosheets, the preparation of cobalt porphyrin MOF, and the hybrid of HBCNN/CoPMOF. The details were as follows:
## 2.1.1. Preparation of $g{-}C_{3}N_{4}$ nanosheets
## 2.1.2. Preparation of boron doped $g_{-}C_{3}N_{4}$ nanosheets and acid treatment
To obtain HBCNN, the BCNN powder was immersed in hydrochloric acid $(1.5\mathrm{M})$ and stirred for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , and then washed with water to neutral, resulting in the HBCNN (HBC for short).
## 2.1.3. Preparation of cobalt porphyrin MOF
Cobalt porphyrin MOF was synthesized by a previously reported method with a minor modification [48]. In detail, tetrakis (4-carboxylphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP, $40\ \mathrm{\mg}$ and benzoic acid $(25~\mathrm{mg})$ were dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of N, N-dimethyl formamide (DMF) with a three-necked flask. Then, the resultant dark violet liquid was heated to $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in an oil-bath pan under magnetic stirring. Subsequently, the cobalt nitrate solution ( $100\ \mathrm{\mg}$ of Co $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ was dissolved in $5\ \mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol) was dropwise added into the three-necked flask within 3 min. The resultant mixture was heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for another $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After naturally cooling, the resultant dark-violet powder was washed with DMF and ethanol for three times, resulting in CoPMOF.
## 2.1.4. Preparation of acidified boron-doped $g{-}C_{3}N_{4}$ nanosheets/cobalt porphyrin MOF
The heterojunction composed of HBCNN and CoPMOF was prepared according to the following steps: Firstly, HBCNN and CoPMOF were dispersed in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water with a certain feeding mass ratio. After ultrasonic treatment for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the mixture was maintained at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ under magnetic stirring. Subsequently, the mixture was washed with deionized water and ethanol for several times. Finally, the resultant product was collected by centrifugation, resulting in the heterojunction composed of HBCNN and CoPMOF, which was labeled as $\mathsf{H B C/C P_{x}}$ for convenience (x was the mass ratio of HBCNN to CoPMOF, ${\bf\nabla}\mathbf{X}=0.8$ , 1.2, 1.6, 2.0, respectively).
## 2.1.5. Preparation of control samples
In the following photocatalytic experiment, we found the HBC/ $\mathsf{C P}_{1.6}$ exhibited the highest hydrogen evolution rate among all samples. To investigate the effect of acidification and boron-doping on the photocatalytic performance, two counter samples, borondoped $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanosheets/cobalt porphyrin MOF (marked as BC/ $\mathsf{C P}_{1.6})$ and acidified $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ nanosheets/cobalt porphyrin MOF (marked as $\mathsf{H C}/\mathsf{C P}_{1.6})$ , were also synthesized by the method similar to $\mathsf{H B C/C P_{x}}$ without acidification process or boron-doping, respectively.
## 2.2. Characterization
The XRD spectrum was measured with a Bruke D2 PHASER instrument. A Lorenz transmission electron microscope (TalosF200X) was used to analyze the microstructure of photocatalysts. The element information was studied with a Fourier transform Infrared spectra (FT-IR). The UV–visible (UV–vis) absorption spectrum was collected with a UV–vis-NIR spectrometer (JASCO, V670) with an integrating sphere. Photoluminescence (PL) spectra were recorded on an Edinburgh Instruments FLS980 fluorescence spectrophotometer. $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopies (XPS) were carried out on an X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (ESCALAB $\mathrm{\Xi^{+}}$ ) with aluminum $\operatorname{K}_{\mathfrak{X}}$ radiation. The photoelectric properties were determined on an electrochemical station (CHI 660E) with a three-electrode quartz cell. Surface photovoltage (SPV) spectroscopy was measured on CEL-SPS100 based on a locking amplifier and a monochromatic light source.
## 2.3. Photocatalytic performance measurement
The photocatalytic water-splitting hydrogen evolution was performed on a top-irradiated quartz cell connected to a closed glass gas circulation system (CEL-SPH2N, Beijing). Typically, $5\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst was dispersed in $25{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ of aqueous solution with $700~\mathrm{{mg}}$ ascorbic acid as sacrificial agent in the presence of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}$ solution $(0.8\mathrm{\:\omegat\%})$ . The suspension was ultrasonicated and evacuated before irradiated by a 225 W xenon arc lamp (CELPF300-T8). Meanwhile, the reaction temperature was kept at $6^{\circ}C$ through a cooling water circulator. The amount of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ produced was detected with a TCD gas chromatograph (GC-9720).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Analysis of morphology, structure and composition
XRD patterns of $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}.$ , HBCNN and $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ are shown in Fig. 1a. The diffraction peaks of $27.5^{\circ}$ and $13^{\circ}$ can be ascribed to (002) and (100) crystal planes of $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ (JCPDS, Card No. 87-1526) [49]. The decreasing peak intensity at $27.5^{\circ}$ $(\mathrm{g}{-}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}>\mathrm{HBCNN}>\mathrm{HBC}/C\mathrm{P}_{1.6})$ indicates the crystallinity of $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ was weakened after the successful acidification process and combination with CoPMOF. The diffraction patterns of CoPMOF, HBC/ $\mathsf{C P}_{1.6}$ and simulated result of CoPMOF are given in the inset in Fig. 1a. The XRD pattern of CoPMOF showed two peaks at $17.8^{\circ}$ and $7.53^{\circ}$ corresponding to (002) and (110) planes, which is consistent with other two dimensional (2D) TCPP MOFs [48,50–51] However, for ${\mathrm{HBC}}/{\mathrm{CP}}_{1.6},$ the broad peak at $17.8^{\circ}$ almost disappeared while the peak at $7.53^{\circ}$ shifted by around $0.44^{\circ}$ , which can be ascribed to the decrease of crystallinity and the change of lattice distance due to the interfacial interaction [48].
To further reveal the bond information of $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ structure, the IR spectra of samples were recorded. As shown in Fig. 1b, the IR spectrum of CoPMOF exhibited a peak at $999~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to Co-N bonds, indicating the successfully inserting of cobalt atoms into the porphyrin center of CoPMOF [48]. The IR spectrum of HBCNN presented three broad peaks at 810 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ,
$900{\sim}1800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3000{\sim}3500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which can be ascribed to the stretching vibration modes of tri-s-triazine (heptazine) rings, aromatic C-N heterocycles and N-H, respectively [47,52]. As for ${\mathrm{HBC}}/{\mathrm{CP}}_{1.6}$ , the Co-N peak slightly right-shifted by around $2~{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ , which can be attributed the reaction between the unsaturated coordinative Co atoms in CoPMOF and the N atoms in HBCNN, inducing a more delocalized and stable ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{II}})$ electronic structure [53]. In addition, other peaks of HBCNN were well retained in the IR curve of ${\mathrm{HBC}}/{\mathrm{CP}}_{1.6},$ revealing the great structure assembly of two components. Furthermore, Zeta potential measurement was performed to identify the surface charge of BCNN, HBCNN and CoPMOF. As illustrated in Fig. S1, CoPMOF and BCNN were both negatively charged with the zeta potential values of $-20.4$ and $-12.3\mathrm{mV}$ , respectively. After the acidification process, the surficial potential of BCNN was tuned from $-12.3~\mathrm{mV}$ to $+18.6~\mathrm{mV}$ which indicates the successful protonation of surface -C-N- sites of $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ [8]. The opposite surface potential of CoPMOF and HBCNN explains the self-assemble behavior of 2D/2D layered structure, which is also confirmed by the subsequent TEM and EDS results.
As displayed in the TEM images (Fig. 2a-d), the CoPMOF exhibited an overlapped and smooth nanosheet-like structure with a width of hundreds of nanometers (Fig. 2a and b). In reverse, HBCNN showed a curved and layer structure (Fig. 2c). In Fig. 2d, $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ presented a stacked structure of both curved nanosheets and smooth nanosheets, demonstrating the coexistence of CoPMOF and HBCNN. To further verify the combination of CoPMOF and HBCNN in ${\mathrm{HBC}}/{\mathrm{CP}}_{1.6}$ , EDS mapping was carried out. As shown in Fig. 2f and Fig. 2g, the characteristic elements of CoPMOF—Co and O showed a concentrated distribution along a line shape at the edge side of CoPMOF while the characteristic B element of HBCNN appeared uniformly (Fig. 2h). This further confirms the successful combination of CoPMOF and HBCNN in $\mathsf{H B C}/\mathsf{C P}_{1.6}$ . In addition, the real contents of boron and Co in $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ were determined by ICP-MS (Table S1).
XPS measurement was employed to investigate the valence states and compositions of as-prepared samples. The survey XPS spectra of HBCNN, CoPMOF and $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ are shown in Fig. 3a. As expected, all the XPS peaks of C, N and O appeared in the spectra of three samples while the Co XPS peak only appeared in the spectra of CoPMOF and $\mathsf{H B C}/\mathsf{C P}_{1.6}$ . As displayed in Fig. 3b, the N 1s spectrum of CoPMOF displayed two peaks at 398.5 and $400.5\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to N-H and Co-N of porphyrin rings, respectively [53–54]. For HBCNN, three peaks appeared at 398.6, 399.6 and $401.0\mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to $C=N-C$ , N- $(\mathsf{C})_{3}$ and $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , respectively [53,55]. The N 1s curve of $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ showed a similar three-peak pattern to that of HBCNN, with a left-shift of $0.3{\sim}0.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ indicating the loss of electrons for N atoms arising from the interfacial coordinative interactions between N from HBCNN and Co from porphyrin rings of CoPMOF. Hocking’s group has evidenced that the unsaturated coordinative Co with electron pair donation from porphyrin N can be pulled out of the porphyrin plane and partially oxidized [55]. Thus, it’s easy for the partially oxidized Co centers to accept the electrons from another kind N of HBCNN and then form a more delocalized N-Co-N structure. For Co 2p high resolution XPS spectrum of CoPMOF shown in Fig. 3c, a $2{\mathsf{p}}3_{/2}$ peak at $780.8\:\mathrm{eV}$ and a satellite peak at $785.6\mathrm{eV}$ were resolved with their corresponding splitting peaks at $796.3\mathrm{eV}$ $(2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ and $802.3\mathrm{eV}$ (satellite splitting). The satellite peaks can be explained by the shake-up process on the $2{\tt p}$ orbital for first-transition metal paramagnetic compounds. For ${\mathrm{HBC}}/{\mathrm{CP}}_{1.6}$ , the Co 2p XPS survey spectrum showed similar four peaks at $780.5(2{\tt p}_{3/2})$ , 785.3 (satellite), 796.0 $(2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ and 802.0 eV (satellite splitting). Compared with CoPMOF, the Co 2p spectrum of $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ shifted down by $0.3~\mathrm{eV}$ , demonstrating the behavior of accepting electrons for Co centers and interfacial
Co-N bonding. In details, the chemical shift between splitting peaks $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ for CoPMOF $\left(\Delta\mathrm{E}_{1}=15.5\mathrm{eV}\right)$ is discussed to obtain the valence information. According to OKAMOTA’s method [56], DE is closer to $16.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\operatorname{Co}(\mathbb{I})$ and $15.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ for ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{III}})$ . $\Delta\mathsf{E}_{1}$ of $15.5\mathrm{eV}$ indicates the coexistence of ${\mathsf{C o}}(\mathbb{I})$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{III}})$ in CoPMOF. Meanwhile, the chemical shift between splitting peaks $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ for $\mathrm{HBC/CP}_{1.6}\left(\Delta\mathrm{E}_{2}\right)$ is still $15.5\mathrm{eV}$ , which means the valence of Co retained after electrostatic assembly. The result further confirms Co from porphyrin rings and N from HBCNN formed coordinative bonds instead of covalent bonds. In addition, physical mixture sample of $\mathrm{HBCNN/CoPMOF}_{1.6}$ through direct grinding was also prepared for comparison. According to Fig. S2a, the partial Co 2p XPS curve of $\mathsf{H B C}/\mathsf{C P}_{1.6}$ physical mixture was similar to that of pristine CoPMOF, indicating molecular interaction between HBCNN and CoPMOF hardly occurred under the condition of physical grinding. However, obvious decline of peak intensity and rightshift of binding energy by ${\sim}0.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ appeared for $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ compared with CoPMOF and physical mixture sample, demonstrating the existence of interaction between HBCNN and CoPMOF during the aqueous electronic self-assembly process. Moreover, the B 1s XPS spectra are shown in Fig. S2b. Only the B 1s XPS spectrum of HBCNN with calcination temperature of $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ presented a weak peak signal at $\sim190.8$ eV attributed to C-NB group [42], which is consistent with the low content of B dopant, as confirmed by ICP-MS result (Table S1). All of the above discussions demonstrate that the HBCNN and CoPMOF have formed a 2D/2D hybrid structure successfully by electrostatic self-assembly and interfacial coordinated Co-N bonds have been constructed for $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$
## 3.2. Optical-electrical properties and photocatalytic hydrogen evolution performance
The optical adsorption properties of samples were characterized by UV–vis absorption spectrum. As shown in Fig. 4a, the UV–vis adsorption spectrum of TCPP ligand exhibited a soret adsorption band at $420\mathrm{nm}$ and four Q bands at $500{\sim}700\mathrm{nm}$ . After it was metallized by Co atoms during the synthetic process of CoPMOF, the soret adsorption declined in the curve of CoPMOF. Moreover, for $\mathsf{H B C}/\mathsf{C P}_{\mathbf{x}}$ hybrid $\mathbf{\check{X}}=0.8$ , 1.2, 1.6, 2.0), the number of $\mathsf{Q}$ bands is reduced to two at 542 and $667~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which can be attributed to the increase of molecular structure symmetry arising from interfacial Co-N bonding[57–58]. For $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ an adsorption edge at $445\ \mathrm{nm}$ was observed, which is consistent with our previous work [59]. After boron-doping, the obtained HBCNN showed a slightly stronger light adsorption in comparison with $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ . In addition, according to previous report, a little increase in the valence band level of $\mathsf{g}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ can be caused by the boron doping [47,59]. That might give an explanation for the optical adsorption change of g$\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ after boron doping—the boron doping can introduce a doping level in the band structure of HBCNN and extend the optical response region slightly. The photos of these samples are showed in Fig. S3.
Fig. 4b shows the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution performances of the samples. The single CoPMOF and pristine HBCNN possessed poor $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rates (0.33 and $10.90{\mathrm{~mmol~g}}^{-1}{\mathrm{~h}}^{-1}$ , respectively). For $\mathsf{H B C/C P_{x}}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{X}}=0.8$ , 1.2, 1.6, 2.0), the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rates showed apparent enhancement compared with single component. Moreover, the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ reached remarkably $33.17~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , which is 100.5 times that of CoPMOF and 3.04 times that of HBCNN, respectively. The superior performance can be attributed to heterojunction formed between HBCNN and CoPMOF, which powerfully promotes the migration and separation efficiencies of charge carriers (the Z-scheme heterojunction will be confirmed subsequently). It should be pointed out that the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rates of the two counter samples $(\mathrm{HC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ and $\mathsf{B C}/\mathbf{CP}_{1.6})$ were measured as 13.50 and $10.86~\mathrm{mmol~g^{-1}~h}$ , respectively, which are significantly lower than the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of ${\mathrm{HBC}}/{\mathrm{CP}}_{1.6}$ implying both acid treatment and boron doping are necessary for enhanced photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution performance of $\mathsf{H B C/C P_{x}}$ heterojunction. The stability of $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ for photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution was also evaluated. The $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution performance of as-prepared photocatalysts in this work was compared with other reported similar photocatalysts for water splitting $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution. As shown in Table S2, although most $\mathsf{M O F}/\mathsf{g}-$ $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ photocatalysts used visible-light irradiation (300 W Xe lamp with $>420~\mathrm{nm}$ filter) as power resource to split water, the photocatalytic performance of this work is rather competitive compared with those irradiated by full light of 300 W Xe lamp. As displayed in Fig. S2c, the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ generation rate showed a slight decline only at the second cycle and maintained in the next three measurements, indicating its good durability. The surface photovoltage (SPV) spectroscopy is an advanced technique that can be directly related to the separation and transfer of photoinduced charge carriers in space. As shown in Fig. 4c, both HBCNN and CoPMOF displayed positive photovoltage signals while the signal of HCBNN was much weaker than that of CoPMOF, indicating they are both n-type semiconductors. The SPV signal of $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ almost disappeared, which may be originated from the capture of photoinduced holes by trap sites of HBCNN.
The PL spectra of the samples were measured to verify the separation behavior of charge carriers. As shown in Fig. 4d, HBCNN displayed a strong signal at $437~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which can be attributed to the serious recombination of charge carriers [60]. After forming the 2D/2D hybrid, the strong peak signal was effectively quenched for ${\mathrm{HBC}}/{\mathrm{CP}}_{1.6},$ implying the great improvement of charge-carrier separation efficiency due to the formed heterojunction. In addition, the PL spectrum of CoPMOF (the inset in Fig. 4d) presented a peak at $645~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and a shoulder peak at $704~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which can be assigned to the excitation of the rich conjugated P-backbone of CoPMOF [61]. To better understand the excitation and migration process during the photocatalytic water splitting reaction, the transient photocurrent and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were carried out. As illustrated in Fig. 4e, both CoPMOF and HBCNN displayed relatively lower photocurrent signals due to the fast recombination of electron-hole pairs. Benefitting from the hybrid structure, the $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ achieved the highest photo-responding current, suggesting that 2D/2D hybrid powerfully accelerates the migration speed of photo-induced charge carriers. In the contrast, two control samples $(\mathsf{B C}/\mathsf{C P}_{1.6}$ and ${\mathrm{HC}}/{\mathrm{CP}}_{1.6})$ performed ordinarily. The results demonstrate two points: (1) Electrostatic attraction is important for the intimate contact between HBCNN and CoPMOF originated from acidification of boron doped $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ , which is the motivation of 2D/2D self-assembly. (2) Boron-doping is also crucial for reducing the recombination of charge carriers. The EIS patterns of HBCNN, CoPMOF and $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ are also exhibited in Fig. 4f, the $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ presented the lowest impedance among the three samples, which means higher transfer efficiency of electron-hole pairs. It can be concluded that the construction of HBCNN/CoPMOF 2D/2D heterojunction greatly improves the separation efficiency of charge carriers, which is consistent with its $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution performance.
## 3.3. Photocatalytic mechanism of HBC/CP composites
To reveal the photocatalytic mechanism of the self-assembly 2D/2D $\mathrm{HBC}/\mathrm{CP}_{1.6}$ , the energy band structure of HBCNN and CoPMOF are decided through Mott-Schottky plots and Tauc plots (Fig. S4). The LUMO value of CoPMOF can be obtained as $-0.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (Vs. NHE) from Fig. S4a. The $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ value of CoPMOF can be estimated as $2.7\mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S4c). Thus, the HOMO value of CoPMOF can be determined as $+1.9{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (Vs. NHE). As shown in Fig. S4b, the $\mathtt{E_{c}}$ value of HBCNN can be calculated as $-0.45\mathrm{~V~}$ (Vs. NHE). The $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ values of $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ and HBCNN are $2.97\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $2.96~\mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S4d). The $\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{v}}$ of HBCNN can be determined as $+2.5{\mathrm{~V}}$ According these results, the schematic illustration of the band level details of CoPMOF and HBCNN can be drawn (Scheme 2), and two possible heterojunction types are proposed.
## 3.4. DFT calculation
To deeply uncover the impact patterns on electron-hole dynamic of interfacial Co-N bonds and boron doping, DFT calculation was employed based on the experimental structure data. Three configurations (Fig. 5) were adopted to investigate the relaxed atomic structures of the heterostructure in ball-and-stick models.
Fig. 6 illustrates the spin resolved electronic densities of states for CNN/CoPMOF heterostructures by using $\mathrm{PBE}+\mathrm{U}$ method. It is easy to conclude that the CNN/CoPMOF heterostructures considered in this work are all metallic in both the parallel and tilt configurations. The predicted electronic densities of states in the down spin channel for all three heterostructures are similar to each other, and the metallic character at the Fermi level is preserved regardless of the interfacial Co-N bonding and B dopant in CNN. Meanwhile, the calculated electronic densities of states of upspin channel are found to be very sensitive to the exact interfacial configuration or B dopant. Obviously, the formation of interfacial Co-N coordination bond increases the electronic states at Fermi level in the up-spin channel significantly, compared to that of CNN/CoPMOF in a parallel configuration, as shown in Fig. 6a. Thus, the interfacial Co-N bond could improve the charge transfer efficiency between CoPMOF substrate and CNN. It is intriguing to observe that when both B dopant and interfacial Co-N bond coexist in CNN/CoPMOF heterostructure in a tilt configuration, the electronic density of states in up-spin channel exhibits a pseudo-gap like behavior. For the same heterostructure, the down-spin channel remains metallic. The formation of a pseudo-gap in the up-spin channel may greatly impact the catalytic performance of BCNN/ CoPMOF heterostructure, compared to un-doped CNN/CoPMOF in both parallel and tilt configurations, because the band gap could give rise a highly desired electron-hole separation mechanism. To further reveal whether such an electron-hole separation mechanism applies to the BCNN/CoPMOF heterostructure in a tilt configuration, the partial densities of states of the selected elements (B, C, N and Co) for the three CNN/CoPMOF heterostructures are shown in Fig. 6b, c and d, respectively. As shown in Fig. 6b and c, the electronic states at the Fermi level for CNN/CoPMOF (parallel) and CNN/CoPMOF (tilt) configurations are mainly ascribed to the atoms in CoPMOF especially the Co atoms. However, for the pseudo-gap predicted for BCNN/CoPMOF (tilt) in the up-spin channel, the top of valence band mainly consists of the valence orbitals of B atoms in BCNN, which can support the proposed boron doping level as shown in Scheme 2. And the bottom of conduction band is dominated by the 3d states of Co atoms in CoPMOF substrate. Thus, the electronic states of BCNN/CoPMOF heterostructure possess an electron-hole separation mechanism in the up-spin channel, and such mechanism is certainly not seen in other two interfacial configurations without B dopant in CNN. To be more specific, the photon excited electrons in BCNN/CoPMOF (tilt) heterostructure are mainly confined in the CoPMOF substrate layer. Meanwhile, the holes left behind in the top of valence band are most-likely to be found in CNN, which was agreed with the SPV results. Therefore, the excellent catalytic performance of HBCNN/CoPMOF heterostructure might be explained in terms of a novel electronhole separation mechanism, which is in good agreement with the experimental results, furthermore confirming the transfer mechanism of photogenerated charge carriers shown in the Scheme 2b.
## 4. Conclusions
Here, a series of 2D/2D self-assembled HBCNN/CoPMOF photocatalysts were successfully fabricated through an electrostatic selfassembly process. The optimal sample with a mass ratio of 1.6 (HBCNN to CoPMOF) showed an excellent $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of 33.47 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ , which was 3.14 and 104.6 times higher than the single HBCNN and CoPMOF, respectively. It was revealed that interfacial Co-N bonds were formed during the electrostatic selfassembly process, which significantly promoted the efficiency of electron-hole separation and transfer. Furthermore, according to DFT calculations, a novel pseudo-gap mechanism was proposed, in which electrons and holes were separately confined at the Co sites of CoPMOF and boron sites of HBCNN, resulting in the formation of Z-scheme heterojunction. This work may provide a unique example on investigating interfacial interactions of 2D/2D structure photocatalysts for enhanced photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution performance [43,44]. Future research will mainly be focused on the influence on $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution of substituting different metal nodes and doping nonmetal.
